        "id": 1119068
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-12T12:55:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, depends how you read the energy_diff function. If you start by considering that we do A LOT of assumptions and approximations in computing the energy with this function ... then perhaps having a filtering function on the computed result which prune out some noise that\u0027s not something you expect the caller to do.\n\nI think the idea here is: if we return a non zero number, we likely trust it\u0027s interesting for the caller.\n\nHowever, a further patch which introduces SchedTune filtering makes this check unused (when SchedTune is compiled in), but still we do a similar check before returning the energy_diff.\n\nThese other bits probably should be done on a different function, instead of using always energy_diff we should use a power_perf_diff() function.\nEventually we can move these filtering in such a function.\n\nWhat do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "13db5061_58e9aa12",
